    summary_60s: 'In economics, utility is a key concept used to understand consumer behavior and market dynamics. It refers to the satisfaction or benefit a consumer derives from consuming goods and services. Utility Utility is the level of satisfaction, pleasure, or usefulness a consumer gains f',
    key_formulas: '',
    pro_tips_95: 'Official JAMB & WAEC Syllabus Concept: Focus on core definitions, formulas, and past questions.',
    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Theory of Consumer Behaviour in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>In economics, <strong> utility</strong> is a key concept used to understand consumer behavior and market dynamics. It refers to the satisfaction or benefit a consumer derives from consuming goods and services.</p><h1 style="margin-left:0px; text-align:center"><strong>Utility</strong></h1><p>Utility is the level of satisfaction, pleasure, or usefulness a consumer gains from consuming a product or service. It shapes consumer decisions and preferences.</p><h2><strong>Approaches to Utility</strong></h2><p><strong>Cardinal Approach</strong>:</p><ul><li>This traditional approach suggests that utility can be measured in quantitative units, known as 'utiles.'.</li><li>It posits that utility is a tangible and calculable entity, allowing for comparisons in specific numerical terms.</li></ul><p><strong>Ordinal Approach</strong>:</p><ul><li>Contrary to the cardinal view, the ordinal approach maintains that utility cannot be precisely measured in numbers.</li><li>Instead, it focuses on ranking consumer preferences and choices, using concepts like indifference curves to illustrate these rankings.</li></ul><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Assumptions of the Cardinal Approach</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Quantitative Measurement</strong>: <ul><li>The cardinal approach assumes utility can be quantitatively measured, allowing consumers to express their satisfaction in numerical terms.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Constant Marginal Utility of Money</strong>: <ul><li>It posits that the marginal utility derived from each additional unit of currency remains constant.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Rational Consumer Behavior</strong>: <ul><li>Consumers are presumed to act rationally, aiming to maximize their utility based on available information and resources.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Influence of Quantity on Total Utility</strong>: <ul><li>Total utility is believed to be directly influenced by the quantity of the commodity consumed.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Consistency in Consumer's Taste</strong>: <ul><li>The approach assumes that a consumer's taste and preferences remain constant over the period of consumption analysis.</li></ul></li></ol><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Types of Utility</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Time Utility</strong> – Value gained from having a product available at the right time. <ul><li><em>Example</em>: A winter coat is more useful in winter than in summer.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Place Utility</strong> – Value gained from availability in a specific place. <ul><li><em>Example</em>: A pen is more useful in a school than in a mechanic’s workshop.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Form Utility</strong> – Value added when a product is transformed into a more useful form. <ul><li><em>Example</em>: Turning raw materials into finished goods.</li></ul></li></ol><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Concepts of Utility</strong></h2><ol><li><strong>Total Utility (TU)</strong><ul><li>The overall satisfaction from consuming all units of a good.</li><li>Formula: <strong> TU = AU × QTY</strong></li><li>Where AU = Average Utility, QTY = Quantity.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Average Utility (AU)</strong><ul><li>Satisfaction derived per unit consumed.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Marginal Utility (MU)</strong><ul><li>The additional satisfaction from consuming one more unit.</li><li>Formula: <strong> MU = ΔTU / ΔQ</strong></li><li>Where ΔTU = change in Total Utility, ΔQ = change in Quantity.</li></ul></li></ol><p><img alt="" src="data:image/com/x3zhEXYsd8scKUSHOhcWv1ddgpsrJnxG14naItoXqSNUqg-of4jsq0ekNlqRgLmDtYgK6z8WaK7xGwDM7oExWeD7q9GaUGWnS6IPa9BNzMblWvqt-M7E2-9o2PDrZupcvSeHDko1c3AM1nYJ1tNnQg;base64,"/><img src="data:image/png;base64," style="height:160px; width:250px"/></p><p><strong>Relationship between TU and MU:</strong></p><p>1. Initially,as consumption increases, total utility rises(TU) but marginal utility (MU) falls</p><p>2. When Total utility(TU )gets to its maximum point, Marginal utility( MU )becomes zero (0)</p><p>3. As Total utility(TU) begins to decline, marginal utility(MU) becomes negative.</p>`
